Subject: Mark `__float128` with a newtype wrapper

Rust has no 128-bit float, so `float_kind_rust_type` renders a `__float128` as
the integer of the same width: a bare `u128`. That is also, and correctly, how
an `unsigned __int128` comes out. One token in the output therefore means
either a 128-bit float or a 128-bit integer, and nothing that survives says
which the header wrote.

The two are not interchangeable. They are passed in different registers, they
are read by different instructions, and arithmetic on one applied to the bits
of the other produces a number rather than an error. A consumer which has to
name the type again - to emit C++, or to pick a Rust type to do arithmetic in -
has to guess, and a wrong guess is wrong at runtime.

`--use-float128-newtype-wrapper` wraps the chosen type in
`__bindgen_marker_Float128<T>`, the same shape `--use-opaque-newtype-wrapper`,
`--use-reference-newtype-wrapper` and `--use-long-double-newtype-wrapper`
already use: a fake type the caller declares itself, through `--raw-line` or by
post-processing. What it wraps is left as it was, so a caller who declares the
marker `#[repr(transparent)]` gets the layout bindgen chose; and the
`unsigned __int128` beside it is left bare, which is what makes the bare `u128`
mean one thing again.

The wrap is at the `TypeKind::Float` arm of `TryToRustTy for Type`, beside the
`long double` wrapper and outside it - a float is one kind or the other, so at
most one marker is ever added. `TypeKind::Complex(FloatKind::Float128)` is left
alone: a `_Complex __float128` is already `__BindgenComplex<T>`, which says on
its face that it is not a plain integer. Off by default, so no existing output
changes.

--- a/codegen/helpers.rs
